UNSUPERVISED LEARNING:

- In contrast to supervised learning, unsupervised learning trains a machine with unlabeled data, such
    as the text of a book. Training an AI system with unlabeled data is more difficult because the
    system can’t make predictions until it has structured the data itself. In the book example,
    structuring the data would involve breaking down the text and finding relationships between words
    and sentences. So, algorithms explore the data and try to find structure. 

- For example, a system might be fed many articles about different kinds of plants and form its own
    conclusions of their attributes. When the system ingests new text describing a plant, it identifies
    it and gives it a confidence value.